Z||000008||gmata||06-08-2000||03:26 PM||||If you want to hear about spending money on your car see the following:<P>I bought a 1986 Fiero GT for $2300 at the end of April.<P>The car when into the shop from April 25 - May 8.  It had $800 dollars for brake work, $300 for the rear seal, $300 for a tune-up and radiator flush plus a transmission flush. $200 to replace the EGR valve and vacumm.<P>Then the car had the muffler replaced for $550.<P>Then..It when to the dealership to get the starter, tach filter, thermostat sensor, oil sensor replaced.  They also check the cluster and concluded that the cluster was for a 4 cylinder instead of a 6 cylinder.  They had to replace the AC relay adapter.  All for $1200.<P>I also bought the following for $600 dollars:<BR>Got the quarter windows, both inner and outer window seals, a front marker, and both outer lenses for the back lights, and the driver side window.<P>At the present time, I spent $4,200 dollars on repairs, $2,300 for the car, and I haven't been able to drive it more than 10 miles in the last two months !!!!<P>Plus I need to replace the stereo( $200), and the rims( $400 ).<P>But the car sure looks good...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||gmata||06-08-2000||03:26 PM||||If you want to hear about spending money on your car see the following:<P>I bought a 1986 Fiero GT for $2300 at the end of April.<P>The car when into the shop from April 25 - May 8.  It had $800 dollars for brake work, $300 for the rear seal, $300 for a tune-up and radiator flush plus a transmission flush. $200 to replace the EGR valve and vacumm.<P>Then the car had the muffler replaced for $550.<P>Then..It when to the dealership to get the starter, tach filter, thermostat sensor, oil sensor replaced.  They also check the cluster and concluded that the cluster was for a 4 cylinder instead of a 6 cylinder.  They had to replace the AC relay adapter.  All for $1200.<P>I also bought the following for $600 dollars:<BR>Got the quarter windows, both inner and outer window seals, a front marker, and both outer lenses for the back lights, and the driver side window.<P>At the present time, I spent $4,200 dollars on repairs, $2,300 for the car, and I haven't been able to drive it more than 10 miles in the last two months !!!!<P>Plus I need to replace the stereo( $200), and the rims( $400 ).<P>But the car sure looks good...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ditz88GT||06-08-2000||03:59 PM||||I have Kicker 4X10's in my Fiero.  I think a couple other companies are starting/still making our size.||||reg||7
Z||000011||Fformula88||06-08-2000||04:07 PM||||I've got a set of JBL 4X10's in mine.  They are not bad.  They sounded a lot better than the Pioneer's that were on display.  I found a pair of Alpine's, but they didn't fit right.  They had a thick ring around the tiop for mounting them under the rear deck of a sedan, and the factory covers would not fit into place.  Those are the best 4X10's I've ever heard.  But at $109, I wasn't about to try and modify them and hope I didn't damage them.  I just wish I could get a little more bass out of the JBL's.<P>As for the $$ spent...<P>So far I have $2080 in repair receipts in three years.  New oil pan gasket, distributor o-ring gasket, various sensors, vacumn leak diagnostics and repairs, EGR tube, tune ups, shocks, and probably a few more minor items.<P>I have also put in JBL 4X10's $79,<P>an Alpine CD head $199, <P>put a factory spoiler on the back (it was missing when I bought the car) $150 and $50 for the paint (I did it myself, and it looks GREAT!)<P>Embroidered Fiero Floor mats with the pegasus shield $100<P>4 new tires, Dunlop d60 A2's $340<P>I guess that puts me close to $3000.  And I am very happy with it!  Averaged out, it is still less per month than my payments on my 97 Probe!||||reg||10
Z||000012||Monkeyman||06-08-2000||05:33 PM||||Formula--"Recovered seats $100"?  Where? How? With what?  Sounds like a great deal!  Why are you using 93 octane gas?  I use the mid grade and don't seem to have a problem with spark knock.<P>Alex4mula--I hate to disappoint you, but you spent too much on your 3.4.  I just talked to <B>the</B> 3.4 guru, Ed Parks, yesterday and he said that he could do a reman 3.<B>6</B> for $3300.  <P>Fformula88--I have a set of Kenwood 4x10s in the Grape Ape.  They have the same problem with the big lip.  I used some other stock Fiero screws.  They were a real b**** to install (the screws), but they're solidly mounted and the cover still fits fine.<P>I'm not even going to get into how much I have invested so far.  :::crying:::  I'm guessing a couple of thousand and I haven't even started.  How much is mucho frustration worth?!?||||reg||1
Z||000013||bHooper||06-08-2000||05:46 PM||||Talk about expensive!  I just brought my baby back from the dead... this is what it's cost, so far:<P>Corsica tranny... $370<BR>work on tranny... $320 (with parts)<BR>new starter...... $ 60<BR>new altenator.... $ 75<BR>new slave cyl.... $140<BR>new clutch/pp.... $120<BR>surface flywheel. $ 30<BR>flywheel bolts... $ 27<BR>various tools.... $185<BR>dogbone bushing.. $ 25<BR>frnt tranny mnt.. $ 20<BR>shift select brkt $ 27<BR>fluids........... $ 65<BR>misc. bolts/fast. $ 40<BR>battery.......... $ 68<BR>fiero store order $ 75 (batt. mnt/crdl bush)<BR><B>total.............$1647[b/]<P>I believe that total is about $400-500 less than it should be!  [b]JUST TO GET THE CAR RUNNING!</B><P>This fall, I plan on swaping for a 3.4 liter, around $2500 total... engine, clutch, manifolds, and exhaust.  Then there is the new custom suspension $700??  Of course, paint... $900 with a friends help, and leather interior... $2000??<P>That comes to $7750!  Add 20% for error... <B>that comes to $9296 FRICKIN DOLLARS!!!</B><P>on second thought... maybe I should get out of the fiero business?<P>nah!!!||||reg||1
Z||000014||rogergarrison||06-09-2000||12:28 PM||||Well guys, I WIN. Since Jan 99, ive put $25,000.00 in mine, give or take a few hundred. Mines converted to a Ferrari look-a-like. $3500.00 for 4 wheel rims, complete real Ferrari interior, just for starters. And its still the V-6, but highly modified. And I think it looks gorgeous, even fools Ferrari guys. And thanks to Rodney got lot of his goodies too, Ferrari shift gate, Ferrari VIN plate and more.<P>RG||||reg||1
Z||000015||Alex4mula||06-09-2000||01:34 PM||||Monkeyman you don't disapoint me. My 3.4 is actually an Ed Parks modified reman. 3.6. The cost I posted includes ceramic coated headers ($730), Fieroshop clutch ($395), TBS porting job ($170), under drive pulleys ($70), sensors ($?), all new mounts($100s), miscelaneous, etc. etc. All that included amount to the $4K. The details takes a lot of $$...||||reg||1
Z||000016||bHooper||06-09-2000||01:47 PM||||Alex, you are correct!  The little things are what add up.  Look at my summary... the large items add up to about $1000, all the little stuff wound up being another $600 or so!<P>And I forgot to add wheels and tires to my wish list.  I expect to pay around $800+1200 just for tires (fancy Bridgstone tires for racing and some nice performance tires for the street), plus whatever the rims are... $1400 may be a decent estiment.<P>But when I'm through, I will have a car that I can take to the track and perform, or just drive to work in.<P>Still, the total will be around $13000... a lot less than a comperable newer or new sportcar!||||reg||1
